<#
    profile-user.ps1 — YOUR half of the shell. NEVER COMMITTED.

    This is a worked example. The real file lives beside your $PROFILE:

        notepad (Join-Path (Split-Path $PROFILE) 'profile-user.ps1')

    Put everything personal here rather than in $PROFILE: aliases, functions,
    environment variables, hostnames. Two reasons — the terminal-help block in
    $PROFILE is rewritten by the installer, and this file is the only one that
    knows anything about your machines.

    terminal-help calls two functions if you define them. Both are optional:

        Show-UserInfo       your own reference sections, printed on demand
        Invoke-UserOnLoad   what runs on every new shell (the ONLY thing that
                            prints at startup besides the version line)

    The Write-Th* helpers are available here, so your sections look like the
    built-in ones: Write-ThHead, Write-ThSub, Write-ThRow, Write-ThNote,
    Write-ThText, Write-ThWarn, Write-ThOk.
#>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 🏷 Aliases and ordinary settings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set-Alias -Name ll -Value Get-ChildItem -Scope Global
# $env:EDITOR = 'code'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 🔧 Your own functions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# function mkcd { param($Path) New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $Path | Set-Location }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 🔒 Your own reference sections
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The things you look up every few weeks and never quite remember: internal
# hostnames, a deploy runbook, the flag with the unmemorable name. This is a
# private file, so it can be as specific as you like.

# function Show-UserInfo {
#     Write-ThHead '🔒' 'My setup'
#     Write-ThRow  'Build box:'    'ssh build-01.internal'
#     Write-ThNote 'only reachable on the VPN'
#     Write-ThRow  'Reset the db:' '.\scripts\db-reset.ps1 -Seed'
#
#     Write-ThSub  '🚀' 'Deploys'
#     Write-ThRow  'Staging:' '.\deploy.ps1 -Environment staging'
#     Write-ThNote 'the flag you always forget goes here, where it is private'
# }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 🏁 What runs on every new shell
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# terminal-help prints only its version line. Whatever else you want at startup
# goes here — and every line of it is a line you read all day.

# function Invoke-UserOnLoad {
#     Write-ThOk (Get-Date -Format 'dddd HH:mm')
# }
